{-
   What a teardown costs - Implementation-Containers.md §13.2.

   `Reclaim(Array(Int))` is the cheapest teardown there is, an element type with nothing to run, and
   it used to compile to a call containing an O(n) loop that does nothing, ending in a second call
   containing a comparison. None of that shows up in a result, which is why this fixture asserts the
   optimized resolve IR: the cost is entirely invisible in a number, which is how it went unnoticed.

   Four things the `.opt.expect` beside this says, and what breaks each:

   - **no loop.** `Int` has no teardown, so the walk over the elements reduces to a read nobody
     reads, and `eliminateDeadLoops` removes what is left. Without it every array's last use is O(n)
     in its length for no reason;
   - **no call.** The reclaim is spliced in at the `drop` rather than called, which is what puts the
     placement test in the frame that placed the run. `Reclaim(Array(a)).reclaim(Int)` does not
     appear in this program at all - it was inlined at its only site and then stopped being reached;
   - **`releaseRun` is not a call either**, which is the memory-typed value parameter: a reader of a
     borrowed container used to be a call whatever its size, so a comparison cost a frame;
   - **no teardown at all**, in `frameOnly` and `boundMutable` alike - no comparison, no `freeHeap`,
     and no branch to hold them. That is the whole of §13.2 arriving, and it took three things: the
     literal builds its run *into* the array's own field rather than into a temporary beside it
     (`resolveArray`), the tag survives the element writes the literal performs through a raw
     pointer (`pointerSafe`), and where a copy does stand in the way it is crossed as a *fact*
     rather than rewritten (`inheritCopy`).

   The three functions differ in exactly one thing each, so a regression names itself:

   - `frameOnly` is the shortest path - construction straight into the binding, nothing between the
     tag and the read of it;
   - `boundMutable` is the mutable binding, which used to allocate a slot and copy the whole array
     into it and now takes the temporary over - see `test/resolve/MutableBind.*` for the rule and
     for the case it must not apply to. Its teardown folds the same way `frameOnly`'s does;
   - `grown` keeps its comparison and is *supposed* to: `push` may have moved the elements to the
     allocator, so which storage the run is in is a real run-time question.

   All three are read-only after construction. A write through a borrow the callee handed back -
   `xs[i] = v`, whose place is rooted in the `&mut` that `getMut` returned - still forgets everything
   known, because a borrow can name storage a copy put somewhere else and `pointerSafe` deliberately
   does not cover that root. So a *mutated* array's tag survives; that is the next thing to take, and
   it wants the borrow-provenance answer rather than another special case.
-}

-- An array that never leaves the frame and never grows: its teardown is nothing at all.
fn frameOnly() -> Int:
    let xs = [10, 20, 30]
    return xs[0] + xs[2] :: Int

-- The same array, grown past its literal's capacity, where the walk is what goes and the test stays.
fn grown() -> Int:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3]
    push(xs, 4)
    push(xs, 5)
    return truncate(xs[0] + length(xs)) :: Int

-- A mutable binding, which now names the storage the literal was built in rather than a copy of it.
-- Nothing writes it, so its teardown folds the same way.
fn boundMutable() -> Int:
    let &xs = [4, 5, 6]
    return xs[1] :: Int

fn main() -> Int = frameOnly() + grown() + boundMutable()
